    pr_ref:  # 64ab
    description: "FOUND 2026-10-30 by the js-module-linker precision audit for WI-halol shape 5 (7 surveys on dev d4bac1048a: knex, ioredis, pm2, nuclear, apollo-server, jotai, hls.js; enumeration ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/jsmod_10302026/).\n\nTHE DEFECT. `require('.')` / `require('..')` / `import x from '.'` resolve to an npm PACKAGE named \".\" or \"..\" (javascript:npm:0-0:..:package), a tier-3 third-party stand-in, instead of the directory's index file. 35 of 7,290 linker resolutions: nuclear '.' 19 and '..' 6, pm2 '..' 5 (examples/misc-examples/*.js -> examples/index.js? read each), apollo-server '..' 4 and '.' 1.\n\nCAUSE, read in code: linkers/js_module.py _is_relative_import returns import_path.startswith(\"./\") or startswith(\"../\"), so the bare \".\" and \"..\" fall through to the npm-package route. Node resolves both as relative paths (the directory, then its index / package.json main).\n\nWHY IT BLOCKS WI-halol: granting js-module-linker ADR-0057 section 14.1's supersedes_consumed_stub would demote the analyzer's import stub beneath these wrong package answers (0020's refusal reason). Fix this, re-run the audit, then grant.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: _is_relative_import also true for \".\" and \"..\" exactly; check the analyzer side (js_ts module hints, _in_tree_module_member, _InTreeDefaultExports) treat them as relative too. Test: '.' and '..' through run_behavior_map reach the index file; a package literally named like a path cannot exist, so no control is lost."  # 64ab
    fields: {}  # 64ab
